WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:01.680
The rocket equation.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:02.638
It's a beautiful thing.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.130
No astronaut launches
for space

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:08.360
with their fingers crossed.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.362
That's not how we
deal with risk.

00:00:13.254 --> 00:00:16.590
What I learned from 21
years as an astronaut

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:22.220
is that the more you
know, the less you fear.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.530
The reason I wanted to
do this MasterClass

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:27.155
is to share the hidden
depths of purpose that

00:00:27.155 --> 00:00:30.990
are behind space exploration.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.530
When the clock hits zero,
start focusing and thinking

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.710
of nothing else but the
moment that you're in.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.200
Study every system on
a spaceship and then

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.870
boil it down to what
I call a one pager.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.390
You've got to be able to solve
your problems in one breath.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.380
You have to push
your lungs forward

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.810
through the drag
of the atmosphere.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.130
Crushed into your chair –&nbsp;

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:56.780
you're like a leaf
in a hurricane.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:00.320
The reason is one
half rho v squared s.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.140
Sixteen times the speed of
sound. As you accelerate

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.660
harder and harder, that
light blue Florida sky

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:07.370
starts to get darker and darker.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:12.520
And then suddenly, black–
and the engines shut off

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.260
and you're weightless.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.200
Turn the handle
and open the hatch.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.480
You pull yourself
out into the universe

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.940
and now suddenly, when
you move your knees,

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:27.300
you can feel the searing
heat of the sun on one side

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.300
and the incredibly
cold emptiness of space

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.650
on the other.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.450
When you're no
longer earthlings,

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.860
how do you navigate?

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.140
You can use the stars.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.620
You can actually see how that
place where you were raised

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.870
fits into the overall
complexity and reality

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.060
of the solar system.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.460
You start to unavoidably
wonder, are we

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.840
alone in the universe or not?

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.850
Our technology is
just good enough now

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.040
that some of you taking
this MasterClass

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.930
are going to fly in space.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.540
Reusable rocket
ships give us access

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:07.180
to explore like we've
never had before.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:10.300
It will be a great moment of
introspection for humanity

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.400
if you're the person who
finds that one little

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.592
fossilized flower on Mars.

00:02:15.592 --> 00:02:19.899
I'm Chris Hadfield, and
this is my MasterClass.

